# Vocabulary preload for <urn:tar:vocab> (spec §5.4).
#
# EDAM is the recommended default for artifact types (D11) but is not vendored in full: this is
# the working subset that IDS artifacts use, so chips render labels without a network call.
# What a write may name is what the registry holds, and what kind it may name it as is the
# class each concept carries — see the concept classes below.

@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix tar:  <https://w3id.org/tar/ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:  <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix dct:  <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

# --- EDAM data types -------------------------------------------------------

<http://edamontology.org/data_2600> a skos:Concept, tar:ArtifactType ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pathway or network" ;
    skos:definition "An EDAM data type. Kept here only so the label resolves if a record cites it; the registry's own RDF payloads use tar's rdf-graph type instead." .

<http://edamontology.org/data_2048> a skos:Concept, tar:ArtifactType ;
    skos:prefLabel "Report" ;
    skos:definition "A human- or machine-readable report produced by an analysis." ;
    tar:defaultMediaType "text/turtle" .

<http://edamontology.org/data_0006> a skos:Concept, tar:ArtifactType ;
    skos:prefLabel "Data" ;
    skos:definition "A generic data artifact of unspecified type." .

# --- EDAM topics -----------------------------------------------------------

<http://edamontology.org/topic_3071> a skos:Concept, tar:LegacyTopic ;
    skos:prefLabel "Data management" .

<http://edamontology.org/topic_0089> a skos:Concept, tar:LegacyTopic ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ontology and terminology" .

<http://edamontology.org/topic_3068> a skos:Concept, tar:LegacyTopic ;
    skos:prefLabel "Literature and language" .

# --- artifact types the registry defines for itself ------------------------
#
# The OpenLineage adapter types a dataset carrying a schema facet, and had been writing this
# IRI without anything anywhere declaring it: the chip rendered "TabularDataset" from the IRI
# tail, and no picker could offer it. Declared here, it is an ordinary term like any other, and
# the write path that mints it passes the same vocabulary check every other write does.

tar:TabularDataset a skos:Concept, tar:ArtifactType ;
    skos:prefLabel "Tabular dataset" ;
    skos:definition "Data in rows and columns with a declared schema — a table, a CSV export, a relational extract." ;
    skos:altLabel "table" ;
    skos:altLabel "CSV" ;
    tar:defaultMediaType "text/csv" .

# --- the tar: vocabulary itself -------------------------------------------
#
# Audited against DCAT 3 / DCAT-AP, DCTERMS, PROV-O, schema.org + Bioschemas, ADMS, VoID,
# DQV, ODRL, FOAF, DOAP, CodeMeta, biotoolsSchema, SKOS, RO-Crate and the EU authority
# tables on 2026-08-30 (docs/specs/2026-08-30-vocabulary-audit.md). Every term kept below
# says what was checked and why nothing standard fit; terms a standard vocabulary does
# cover are marked owl:deprecated and are no longer written, only read as fallbacks for
# graphs written before the audit.

# ---- concept classes ------------------------------------------------------
#
# A vocabulary-valued field needs more than a term that exists. Pointed at this registry and
# told to classify a tool, a real coding agent produced `edamontology.org/topic_3170`, which
# does exist — it is EDAM's "RNA-Seq" — and an existence check waved it onto a record that had
# nothing to do with RNA-Seq. So every write asks which *kind* of concept it was handed.
#
# That kind used to be a `tar:conceptBranch` string written beside the concept, and the string
# came apart from the concept the first time the two were written by different code paths: the
# registry's own types were created by the type handler into <urn:tar:local>, a later backfill
# put their markers into <urn:tar:vocab>, and every query asking for both inside one GRAPH
# block then found neither. Those types stayed held and accepted on write while no picker would
# offer them, which reads as "this registry has no types of its own".
#
# So the kind is a class now, asserted in the same statement that makes the concept. A marker
# that *is* the `a skos:Concept` statement cannot be written into a different graph from it,
# and rdfs:subClassOf keeps every one of these a skos:Concept to a reader that has never heard
# of tar:.

tar:ArtifactType a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Artifact type" ;
    rdfs:comment "What an artifact *is*: the range of dct:conformsTo on an artifact, and of tar:produces and tar:consumes on a capability. Every capability query and every subscription filter matches this exactly, so a near-synonym costs more here than anywhere else in the model — which is why a value that is not one of these is refused rather than stored as the free IRI the spec originally allowed." .

tar:ResearchTopic a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Research topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "A field of science a Software record is about: the range of dct:subject. A separate class from tar:ArtifactType because the two are not interchangeable and the confusion is the common one — a subject area put in conforms_to describes nothing, and without distinct kinds the registry has no way to notice." .

tar:ArtifactKeyword a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Artifact keyword" ;
    rdfs:comment "A concept in the registry's own artifact-keyword scheme. A keyword is a label rather than a type, so it must never be accepted where a type or a topic is expected; it is a class rather than a bare member of the scheme so the pickers scope to it with the same query they use for every other kind, knowing nothing about that scheme in particular." .

tar:LegacyTopic a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Legacy topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "A subject-area concept from the vocabulary this registry classified software by before the current one, bundled only so a record that already cites one still renders a label. Never offered by a picker and never accepted on a write — this is the class `topic_3170` belongs to. Deliberately its own class rather than an untyped skos:Concept: a term cached from a peer is also untyped, and a peer is authoritative for its own types where this is a term nobody should choose again, so the two must not collapse into one." .

# ---- marker classes -------------------------------------------------------

tar:Software a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Software" ;
    rdfs:comment "Registry-internal discriminator beside schema:SoftwareApplication. Kept: a Release is also a schema:SoftwareApplication (spec §4.2), so SHACL targeting and count queries need a type that separates the two; no standard vocabulary distinguishes 'abstract software' from 'versioned release' as classes." .

tar:Release a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Release" ;
    rdfs:comment "Registry-internal discriminator for a versioned, runnable plan (also schema:SoftwareApplication + prov:Plan). Kept for the same SHACL-targeting reason as tar:Software." .

tar:Instance a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Instance" ;
    rdfs:comment "Registry-internal discriminator for a deployment (also prov:SoftwareAgent, and dcat:DataService when it serves an endpoint). Kept: prov:SoftwareAgent alone cannot be a SHACL target without also firing on other agents." .

tar:Capability a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "A class-level declaration of what a Software, Release or Instance can consume and produce. Kept after checking Bioschemas ComputationalTool (bioschemas.org/properties/input|output expect FormalParameter nodes attached to the tool itself, not a reusable declaration object) and biotoolsSchema (function/input/output is JSON/XML, not RDF). The /export/biotools endpoint is where that mapping belongs; in the graph the Capability node doubles as the prov:Plan of runs." .

# ---- capability -----------------------------------------------------------

tar:hasCapability a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "has capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "Software/Release/Instance to Capability. Kept: no standard property attaches an I/O declaration object to a tool (schema:featureList is free text; bioschemas input/output skip the declaration node entirely)." .

tar:produces a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "produces" ;
    rdfs:domain tar:Capability ;
    rdfs:comment "Capability to ArtifactType (any IRI; EDAM recommended). Kept: bioschemas:output expects a FormalParameter with name/encodingFormat, not a bare type concept; wfdesc:hasOutput expects workflow Parameter nodes. Both would force an indirection this model does not need — the EDAM typing harvesters care about is already here as the object IRI." .

tar:consumes a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "consumes" ;
    rdfs:domain tar:Capability ;
    rdfs:comment "Capability to ArtifactType. Kept for the same reasons as tar:produces (bioschemas:input / wfdesc:hasInput expect parameter nodes, not type concepts)." .

# ---- structure ------------------------------------------------------------

tar:runsRelease a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "runs release" ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a deployment to the versioned plan it currently executes. Kept: checked schema.org (no 'runs version of' relation), DOAP (doap:release goes Project→Version, the wrong direction and wrong subject), PROV (prov:hadPlan exists only on a qualified Association), DCAT (dcat:version is a literal). Nothing standard expresses 'deployment X currently runs release Y'." .

tar:instanceOf a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "instance of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Denormalised link from a deployment to its Software, derivable through the Release. Kept: checked schema.org, DOAP, DCAT and PROV — no vocabulary has a 'deployment of software' property (dct:isVersionOf would wrongly claim the Instance is a version, prov:specializationOf wrongly claims it is the same entity)." .

tar:usedRelease a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "used release" ;
    rdfs:comment "Denormalised Run-to-Release shortcut; the authoritative form is prov:qualifiedAssociation/prov:hadPlan, always written beside it. Kept: PROV deliberately has no unqualified activity-to-plan property, and prov:used cannot be reused because this registry's readers treat prov:used as 'consumed artifact' (lineage traversal, used_count)." .

# ---- access descriptors ---------------------------------------------------

tar:availability a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "availability" ;
    rdfs:comment "public | restricted | embargoed | metadata-only. Kept, but always written beside its standard reading: dct:accessRights with the EU access-right authority table (PUBLIC / RESTRICTED / NON_PUBLIC), which is what DCAT-AP harvesters understand. The table has no embargo concept and cannot distinguish 'described but not retrievable' from merely non-public, and the metadata-only SHACL rules key on those distinctions, so the four-way literal remains authoritative and dct:accessRights is the lossy supplement (embargoed and metadata-only both coarsen to NON_PUBLIC)." .

tar:accessProtocol a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "access protocol" ;
    rdfs:comment "https | s3 | sparql | oci | ipfs | file. Kept: DCAT 3 conveys protocol only implicitly via the accessURL scheme and dcat:accessService/endpointDescription; there is no machine-readable protocol property in DCAT, DCAT-AP, VoID or schema.org. An explicit enum lets a client pick a retrievable distribution without URL-scheme heuristics." .

tar:authMethod a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "auth method" ;
    rdfs:comment "none | apikey | oauth2 | basic | signed-url. Kept: checked DCAT 3 (nothing), ODRL (policies express permissions/duties, not authentication mechanics), schema:conditionsOfAccess (free text for humans). Machine-readable auth schemes otherwise live only inside OpenAPI documents behind dcat:endpointDescription; this is the FAIR A1.2 hint at the distribution itself." .

tar:accessRequestURL a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "access request URL" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where to request access when a distribution is not public. Kept: nearest standard terms are dcat:landingPage (dataset-level, general navigation, not specifically an access-request flow) and odrl:hasPolicy (expresses the policy, not where to apply). DCAT-AP 3 has no access-request property; HealthDCAT-AP is still drafting one. Revisit when that stabilises." .

tar:defaultMediaType a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "default media type" ;
    rdfs:comment "Annotates an ArtifactType concept with the media type its artifacts usually have. Kept: dcat:mediaType is defined on dcat:Distribution (each concrete distribution here already carries it); putting it on a skos:Concept would misstate its domain. This is scheme-level UI metadata, not a claim about any distribution." .

# ---- run bookkeeping ------------------------------------------------------

tar:status a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "run status" ;
    rdfs:comment "success | failed | running | aborted. Kept, but always written beside schema:actionStatus (runs are also typed schema:Action): the schema.org ActionStatusType enumeration has no distinct member for 'aborted' (folded into FailedActionStatus), so the literal stays authoritative and the schema term is the interoperable supplement. PROV models run state only indirectly (prov:endedAtTime plus subtyping), which loses failure semantics." .

tar:openLineagePayload a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "OpenLineage payload" ;
    rdfs:comment "The original OpenLineage event, preserved verbatim so nothing the mapping does not name is lost. Kept: OpenLineage is JSON, not RDF; no vocabulary models 'the foreign event this record was derived from' better than a literal." .

tar:claimedNamespace a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "claimed namespace" ;
    rdfs:comment "The job.namespace an OpenLineage payload claimed. Recorded as a label only — authorisation always comes from the credential (spec §8.3). Kept: deliberately not dct:identifier, which would suggest the value identifies something." .

# ---- lifecycle ------------------------------------------------------------

tar:tombstoned a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "tombstoned" ;
    rdfs:comment "Boolean soft-delete marker; the IRI keeps resolving (spec §7.2). Kept as the cheap query key, but every tombstone also writes the standard reading: adms:status pointing at the EU dataset-status concept WITHDRAWN. prov:invalidatedAtTime was rejected as the sole form because its domain is prov:Entity and tombstones also apply to Instances (agents) and Software." .

tar:tombstonedAt a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "tombstoned at" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the record was tombstoned. Kept alongside adms:status for the same domain reason as tar:tombstoned; on artifacts (prov:Entity) prov:invalidatedAtTime would fit and may be added as a supplement later." .

tar:health a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "health" ;
    rdfs:comment "Operational health of a deployment (ok | degraded | down | unknown), volatile and registry-observed. Kept: no catalogue vocabulary models liveness; DQV measures dataset quality, not service uptime." .

# ---- software description -------------------------------------------------

tar:deployable a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "deployable" ;
    rdfs:comment "False when the software cannot be hosted at an endpoint — a desktop application or a CLI. Such software still has Instances, because a run has to be attributable to something, but those are installations and carry no dcat:endpointURL. Checked against schema.org (applicationCategory and the SoftwareApplication subtypes describe what a tool is, not whether it can be hosted), DOAP and ADMS: nothing expresses hostability, so this stays a tar: term." .

tar:readme a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "readme" ;
    rdfs:comment "The full README of a Software record, as inline Markdown. Kept: codemeta:readme is a URL pointing at a README file, not its content, and schema:text would claim the README is the text of the software record itself. The registry stores the content so the UI renders without a network call." .

tar:healthEndpoint a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "health endpoint" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the registry probes for liveness, when that is not the endpoint URL itself. A declared health endpoint is held to 2xx; without one the endpoint root is probed and judged loosely, since healthy services routinely answer 401 or 404 there. Kept: neither DCAT nor schema.org has a term for a liveness probe, and dcat:endpointURL is the service, not the check." .

tar:selfRegisteredBy a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "self registered by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The credential subject that registered this deployment through PUT /api/v1/instances/self. Written by the registry, never accepted from a caller: with tar:instanceKey it is how a later announcement finds this record instead of creating another." .

tar:instanceKey a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "instance key" ;
    rdfs:comment "The stable name a self-registering deployment calls itself — a hostname, a cluster, a namespace. Distinguishes deployments that share one auto-registration credential." .

tar:registrationClient a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "registration client" ;
    rdfs:comment "An OIDC client id allowed to register deployments of this software for itself. Deliberately not tar:oidcClientId, which on an Instance means the credential *is* that deployment: being a thing and being allowed to create it are different permissions." .

tar:producingSystem a prov:Role ;
    rdfs:label "producing system" ;
    rdfs:comment "The role of the software agent a caller says produced an artifact. Carried on a prov:Attribution rather than by a bare prov:wasAttributedTo, because the registry writes that one itself from the presenting credential and reads it back as a single value — a caller-supplied agent there would make the one attribution nobody can forge indistinguishable from the ones anybody can. Normally the run answers this question and answers it better; this is for artifacts that have no run." .

tar:producingUser a prov:Role ;
    rdfs:label "producing user" ;
    rdfs:comment "The role of the person or agent on whose behalf an artifact was produced. Distinct from dct:creator, which is authorship in the sense that survives into a citation: this is who was at the keyboard, or which account an agent acted under, and it is asked when something needs explaining rather than crediting." .

tar:apiFormat a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "api format" ;
    rdfs:comment "openapi | asyncapi | graphql | sparql-service-description | ols4 | postman | other. Kept beside dct:conformsTo, which carries the specification's own IRI: the short token is what a UI switches on and what a client filters by, and deriving it from an IRI every time is a lookup table in every consumer instead of one triple here." .

tar:readmeBaseURL a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "readme base URL" ;
    rdfs:comment "Base against which relative links and images inside the README resolve, normally a repository's raw content root. Kept: registry-specific rendering detail with no standard counterpart." .

tar:containerImage a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "container image" ;
    rdfs:comment "OCI image reference of a Release (e.g. ghcr.io/org/tool:2.1.0). Kept: checked CodeMeta (nothing for container refs), schema:downloadUrl (an OCI reference is not a URL) and DOAP. Paired with tar:imageDigest for provenance." .

tar:imageDigest a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "image digest" ;
    rdfs:comment "The immutable sha256 digest of the container image. Kept: spdx:checksum applies to a spdx:Checksum node on a distribution's bytes; the digest here identifies the runnable plan, not an advertised artifact." .

tar:installCommand a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "install command" ;
    rdfs:comment "One-line install/pull command for a Release. Kept: codemeta:buildInstructions is a URL to build docs, not an executable one-liner; no schema.org term exists." .

tar:jurisdiction a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "jurisdiction" ;
    rdfs:comment "Legal jurisdiction a deployment operates under (e.g. NL). Kept: dct:spatial asserts spatial *coverage* of data, not the law an operator answers to; DPV's dpv:hasJurisdiction expects a Location resource and would drag in a policy framework for one field. Values stay ISO 3166 literals." .

# ---- workload identity (addendum D12–D15) --------------------------------

tar:oidcClientId a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "OIDC client id" ;
    rdfs:comment "The identity-provider client (Keycloak client, Kubernetes ServiceAccount subject, GitHub Actions subject) allowed to advertise as this deployment. Kept: registry security configuration; no RDF vocabulary models workload-identity binding." .

tar:oidcIssuer a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "OIDC issuer" ;
    rdfs:comment "Restricts the client id above to one issuer; a client id is only unique within an issuer. Kept with tar:oidcClientId." .

tar:allowedScope a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "allowed scope" ;
    rdfs:comment "Scopes granted to a verified workload token that carries none of this registry's own scopes. Kept with tar:oidcClientId." .

# ---- validation reporting -------------------------------------------------

tar:jsonField a rdf:Property ;
    rdfs:label "JSON field" ;
    rdfs:comment "On a sh:ValidationResult: the dotted path into the request body the result is about, so the UI can attach an error to an input. An extra triple on a standard report; plain SHACL consumers ignore it." .

# ---- deprecated: replaced by standard terms in the 2026-08-30 audit -------
# Still read as fallbacks for graphs written before the audit; never written again.

tar:atInstance a rdf:Property ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:label "at instance" ;
    rdfs:comment "Replaced by prov:wasAssociatedWith — PROV-O's own unqualified form of the prov:qualifiedAssociation this registry already writes (domain prov:Activity, range prov:Agent; the Instance is a prov:SoftwareAgent)." .

tar:externalKey a rdf:Property ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:label "external key" ;
    rdfs:comment "Replaced by dct:identifier: 'an unambiguous reference to the resource within a given context' is exactly a CI system's own run/dataset key. adms:identifier was considered and rejected as requiring an adms:Identifier node with agency and notation for what is a plain opaque token." .

tar:homeRegistry a rdf:Property ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:label "home registry" ;
    rdfs:comment "Replaced by dcat:inCatalog (new in DCAT 3, inverse of dcat:resource). Per its scope note the catalog-side dcat:resource edge is written too." .

tar:tagline a rdf:Property ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:label "tagline" ;
    rdfs:comment "Replaced by dct:abstract ('a summary of the resource'); schema:description keeps the long form. schema:slogan was rejected (domainIncludes Organization/Brand/Product, not CreativeWork)." .

tar:kind a rdf:Property ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:label "kind" ;
    rdfs:comment "Replaced by schema:applicationCategory, which the registry already wrote with the identical value — the tar: triple was pure duplication." .

tar:maturity a rdf:Property ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:label "maturity" ;
    rdfs:comment "Replaced by codemeta:developmentStatus ('description of development status, e.g. active, inactive, suspended', repostatus.org values recommended). adms:status was rejected: it expects workflow-status concepts, not software lifecycle." .

tar:contact a rdf:Property ;
    owl:deprecated true ;
    rdfs:label "contact" ;
    rdfs:comment "Replaced by codemeta:maintainer ('individual responsible for maintaining the software, usually includes an email contact address'). schema:maintainer is still pending in schema.org; dcat:contactPoint formally ranges over vcard:Kind while these agents are schema:Person/Organization." .
